          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "Blood pressure is measured as two numbers: systolic pressure (when the heart contracts) over diastolic pressure (between beats), both in millimeters of mercury [diagnostic -- condition-hypertension]. For an accurate reading, a validated device and a cuff sized for the arm should be used [diagnostic -- guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets]. The person should be seated with their back supported and feet flat, their arm supported at heart level, and they should have avoided caffeine, exercise, or smoking in the 30 minutes prior [diagnostic -- guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets]. They should also rest for five minutes beforehand and avoid talking during the measurement [diagnostic -- guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets], as described in the \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\" guideline.\n\nMultiple readings are necessary because blood pressure naturally changes throughout the day due to factors like activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep, and time of day [diagnostic -- condition-hypertension]. It is usually lowest during sleep and rises before waking [diagnostic -- condition-hypertension]. Because of these variations, a single high reading is not enough to diagnose a condition like hypertension, which requires persistently raised pressure over time [diagnostic -- condition-hypertension], as explained in the \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\" document. A diagnosis of hypertension is based on repeated measurements over time, rather than on a single reading [consultation -- condition-hypertension]. A single high reading in a clinic is not enough for a diagnosis [consultation -- condition-hypertension].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
